Phase 1: Handling the Audio Engine (scsynth)
scsynth requires a low-latency hardware audio API to interact with the device's audio hardware. On desktop Linux, this is typically handled by JACK or ALSA. 
scsynth +1

    The Android Solution: You must map the audio backend to either AAudio (modern, low-latency C API) or Oboe (Google’s recommended C++ wrapper that automatically chooses the best native path).
    The Code Change: You will need to write or adapt an audio driver implementation inside SuperCollider's source code (common/Source/server/) that pipes the synthesis loop buffer directly into the AAudio/Oboe callbacks. 
    scsynth

Phase 2: Building the Components
You will configure the build system by disabling all graphical dependencies. 
scsynth

    Pass CMake Flags: Use flags like -DSC_QT=OFF, -DNO_X11=ON, and -DSC_IDE=OFF. 
    scsynth
    Cross-Compile Dependencies: SuperCollider relies heavily on external libraries like Boost, FFTW3, and libsndfile. You must cross-compile these for Android's architecture (arm64-v8a) first, or configure the CMake file to pull pre-compiled Android binaries for them. 
    GitHub +3
    Compile as Shared Libraries: Modify the build targets so they produce .so libraries instead of standalone CLI binaries, ensuring they can be packed into an Android APK.

Phase 3: The JNI and Socket Layer
Because sclang and scsynth naturally communicate over localhost network sockets (typically via UDP ports 57110 and 57120), you do not need complex JNI logic to pass audio data or control signals between them. The networking layer handles that out of the box. 
GitHub +1
Instead, your JNI Wrapper only needs to handle the lifecycle and process startup:

    Java_com_example_app_SCServer_startServer(...) -> Calls scsynth's entry function, setting up the socket listeners and initializing the audio driver.
    Java_com_example_app_SCClient_compileCode(...) -> Feeds strings of SuperCollider code into the sclang interpreter runtime.

